{"kind":"artifact","text":"The wire-type parity gate at internal/wireparity on exact head be1564d794949566c99d8ed6d89f5ed8fabf2f12. It is an ordinary go test, so it runs in CI wherever the suite runs and needs no separate wiring.\n\nFor each Go wire type it takes the field names encoding/json actually emits — resolving tags, omitempty and unexported fields the way the encoder does, then round-tripping through Marshal so a malformed tag cannot pass by being misparsed twice the same way — and compares them with the names the matching TypeScript interface declares. It reports the difference in both directions rather than only that they differ.\n\nIt compares names, not types, deliberately. Names are the failure that is silent: a renamed or dropped json tag turns a working read into undefined at run time, and TypeScript cannot catch it because the hand-written mirror is the only thing it checks against.\n\nThe one gap it cannot close by itself is stated where the gap is: the Go-type-to-interface pairs live in a single map beside the types they guard, so adding a wire type without adding it there leaves that type unguarded. I chose an explicit map over reflection over the package because a silent miss is exactly what this gate exists to prevent, and an explicit list fails loudly when someone adds a type without thinking about the mirror.\n\nIt rests on beb83d6f, the mirror it guards.","body":{"commit":"be1564d794949566c99d8ed6d89f5ed8fabf2f12","path":"internal/wireparity"}}